  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 137 AM MDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nActive weather pattern likely to continue Sunday into early \nnext week as Pacific jet energy and an associated upper level \ndisturbance moves northeast across Colorado and Wyoming later on\nMonday. Models show an 100 knot jet max lifting north across \nthe Pacific NW region and mainly near the Wyoming and Montana \nborder Sunday afternoon. This jet is associated with a pretty \nstrong upper level trough moving eastward near the US and \nCanadian border, which may bring some early season snowfall to \nthe mountains across Montana and the Yellowstone area. This jet \nmax is forecast to clip the area with models showing decent \nupper level difluence. Convective parameters looks suitable for\nwidely scattered or scattered thunderstorms along the I-80 \ncorridor, and more isolated north of I-80 as some drier air \nenters Carbon, Converse, and Niobrara county. MLCAPE between 400\nto 800 j/kg and straight hodographs with 30-40 knots of shear \nfavor linear segments and locally strong gusty winds. MLCAPE and\nSBCAPE are closer to 1500 j/kg across portions of western \nNebraska, so the central and southern Panhandle have the higher\npotential to see a few strong to severe thunderstorms Sunday \nafternoon and evening. Sunday afternoon will also be pretty \nwindy with the upper level trough not far too our north...expect\nwind gusts of 35 to 45 MPH, mainly north of I-80. For Monday, \nsevere weather is not expected with lower CAPE values and too \nmuch midlevel shear (60+ knots). However, with an upper level \ndisturbance lifting northeast out of the desert SW, another \nround of showers and thunderstorms are possible. This will \ngreatly depend on the speed and location of the disturbance. The\nECMWF is currently more progressive with the shortwave feature,\nshowing it moving into the area nearly 6 to 12 hours earlier \ncompared to the GFS and Canadian. Increased POP up to 50% for \nthe I-80 corridor with ensembles showing some pretty good total \nQPF amounts, but kept POP lower further north towards the North \nPlatte River Valley. Temperatures will be seasonable with highs \nin the upper 70s to upper 80s, but also noticeably cooler \ncompared Friday and Saturday.\n\nFor the rest of next week, all models show southwest flow aloft\ndeveloping over the Front Range as a series of late summer fall-\nlike cold fronts move into the Pacific NW coast and as far south\nas northern California. Not expecting this cooler air to make \nit into Wyoming this early, but breezy conditions are expected, \nespecially across southeast Wyoming, with temperatures a few \ndegrees above average for early September with highs in the mid \n70s to mid 80s for southeast Wyoming, and around 90 for western \nNebraska. Precipitation is uncertain since models are struggling\nto resolve a pair of shortwaves on the southeastern quadrant of\nthe main upper level trough near the Pacific coastline. With \ntiming highly uncertain, POP were kept between 10 to 20 percent.\nEnsembles do not show PWATs lowering substantially next week, \nso can't rule out at least isolated convection across the area.",
